1. A height-adjustable equipment platform, comprising:

a base frame;

an equipment deck;

at least three upright guide towers carried by the base frame;

a plurality of posts carried by the equipment deck, one post sliding vertically in each guide tower;

a rack carried by each of the posts and extending along that post;

a pinion mounted in each guide tower and meshing with the rack of the post sliding in that guide tower, whereby rotation of the pinion raises and lowers that post;

a synchronizing transmission coupling the pinions of the guide towers for rotation together;

a rotary input comprising a worm input shaft and a worm wheel driven by the worm input shaft and fixed to a transverse shaft of the synchronizing transmission, the rotary input being arranged to drive the synchronizing transmission, whereby rotation of the rotary input moves every post by the same distance and the equipment deck remains parallel to the base frame;

a locking pawl carried by each guide tower and mounted to pivot between a locked position engaging the rack of the post sliding in that guide tower and a released position clear of that rack;

a compression spring biasing each locking pawl toward the locked position, a load of the equipment deck at each post being carried through the locking pawl carried by the guide tower in which that post slides, with that locking pawl in the locked position, and through that guide tower;

a release balance bar mounted on the base frame;

a release cable connecting the release balance bar to each locking pawl; and

a release handle acting on the release balance bar, whereby pulling the release handle tensions the release cables and moves all of the locking pawls to the released positions;

wherein the release balance bar is pivotable relative to the release handle, whereby a difference in effective length between the release cables is taken up by pivoting of the release balance bar and no locking pawl remains engaged with its rack while the remaining locking pawls are in the released positions.

2. The platform of claim 1, wherein a mesh between the worm input shaft and the worm wheel resists back-driving of the synchronizing transmission by the load of the equipment deck while the locking pawls in the locked positions carry that load.

3. The platform of claim 1, wherein the base frame is rectangular, the guide towers are four upright guide towers, one near each corner of the base frame, and each post is a corner post depending from a corner of the equipment deck.

4. The platform of claim 3, wherein the synchronizing transmission comprises a front transverse shaft to which two of the pinions are fixed, a rear transverse shaft to which the other two of the pinions are fixed, a front sprocket fixed to the front transverse shaft, a rear sprocket fixed to the rear transverse shaft, and one endless synchronizing chain running over the front sprocket and the rear sprocket, the worm wheel being fixed to the front transverse shaft.

5. The platform of claim 3, wherein the synchronizing transmission comprises a front transverse shaft to which two of the pinions are fixed, a rear transverse shaft to which the other two of the pinions are fixed, and one of a toothed belt coupling the front transverse shaft to the rear transverse shaft, and a train of gears coupling the front transverse shaft to the rear transverse shaft.

6. The platform of claim 1, wherein the rack of each post lies along a side of that post facing an interior of the base frame.

7. The platform of claim 1, wherein each locking pawl carries a locked-state flag and each guide tower has a window positioned such that the locked-state flag of the locking pawl carried by that guide tower is visible through the window only when that locking pawl is fully engaged with a tooth of the rack of the post sliding in that guide tower.

8. The platform of claim 1, further comprising a crank socket coupled to the rotary input, a removable hand crank received in the crank socket, and a mechanical interlock on the crank socket, the mechanical interlock blocking insertion of the hand crank into the crank socket unless the release handle is in a pulled position and blocking return of the release handle from the pulled position until the hand crank has been removed from the crank socket.

9. The platform of claim 1, further comprising a motor coupled to the worm input shaft and arranged to drive the synchronizing transmission through the rotary input.

10. The platform of claim 1, further comprising a cable adjuster in at least one of the release cables.

11. The platform of claim 10, further comprising a removable cover, removal of the removable cover giving access to the synchronizing transmission, the locking pawls, and the cable adjuster without removal of the equipment deck.

12. The platform of claim 1, wherein each locking pawl is mounted on a pawl pivot fixed in the guide tower carrying that locking pawl, and the load of the equipment deck at each post passes from the post through the locking pawl in the locked position and into the guide tower and the base frame, rather than through the synchronizing transmission alone.

13. A height-adjustable equipment platform, comprising:

a base frame;

an equipment deck;

at least three upright guide towers carried by the base frame;

a plurality of posts carried by the equipment deck, one post sliding vertically in each guide tower;

a rack carried by each of the posts;

a pinion mounted in each guide tower and meshing with the rack of the post sliding in that guide tower;

a synchronizing transmission coupling the pinions of the guide towers for rotation together;

a rotary input comprising a worm input shaft and a worm wheel driven by the worm input shaft and fixed to a transverse shaft of the synchronizing transmission, the rotary input being arranged to drive the synchronizing transmission, whereby rotation of the rotary input moves every post by the same distance and the equipment deck remains parallel to the base frame;

a crank socket coupled to the rotary input;

a removable hand crank received in the crank socket;

a locking pawl carried by each guide tower and mounted to pivot between a locked position engaging the rack of the post sliding in that guide tower and a released position clear of that rack, and a compression spring biasing each locking pawl toward the locked position;

a release balance bar mounted on the base frame, a release cable connecting the release balance bar to each locking pawl, and a release handle acting on the release balance bar and movable to a pulled position in which all of the locking pawls are held in the released positions, the compression springs returning the locking pawls to the locked positions when the release handle leaves the pulled position;

a locked-state flag carried by each locking pawl and a window in each guide tower, the window of each guide tower being positioned such that the locked-state flag of the locking pawl carried by that guide tower is visible through that window only when that locking pawl is fully engaged with a tooth of the rack of the post sliding in that guide tower; and

a mechanical interlock on the crank socket, the mechanical interlock blocking insertion of the hand crank into the crank socket unless the release handle is in the pulled position and blocking return of the release handle from the pulled position until the hand crank has been removed from the crank socket.

14. The platform of claim 13, wherein the release balance bar is pivotable relative to the release handle such that a difference in effective length between the release cables is taken up by pivoting of the release balance bar.

15. The platform of claim 13, wherein the worm input shaft is coupled to the crank socket, and a mesh between the worm input shaft and the worm wheel resists back-driving of the synchronizing transmission by a load of the equipment deck while the locking pawls in the locked positions carry that load.

16. A method of adjusting the height of an equipment deck of a height-adjustable equipment platform that comprises a base frame carrying at least three upright guide towers, a plurality of posts carried by the equipment deck, one post sliding vertically in each guide tower, a rack carried by each of the posts, a pinion in each guide tower meshing with the rack of the post sliding in that guide tower, a synchronizing transmission coupling the pinions for rotation together, a rotary input comprising a worm input shaft and a worm wheel driven by the worm input shaft and fixed to a transverse shaft of the synchronizing transmission and driving the synchronizing transmission, a locking pawl carried by each guide tower and biased by a compression spring toward a locked position engaging the rack of the post sliding in that guide tower, a release balance bar connected to the locking pawls by respective release cables, and a release handle acting on the release balance bar, the method comprising:

pulling and holding the release handle so as to tension the release cables and move all of the locking pawls clear of the racks, a difference in effective length between the release cables being taken up by pivoting of the release balance bar relative to the release handle;

while the release handle is held, turning the rotary input so as to move all of the posts by the same distance, the equipment deck remaining parallel to the base frame while the posts move;

releasing the release handle so that each compression spring returns its locking pawl into engagement with a nearest tooth of the rack of the post sliding in the guide tower carrying that locking pawl; and

supporting a load on the equipment deck through the locking pawls engaged with the racks and through the guide towers.

17. The method of claim 16, wherein the platform further comprises a locked-state flag carried by each locking pawl and a window in each guide tower through which the locked-state flag of the locking pawl carried by that guide tower is visible only when that locking pawl is fully engaged with a tooth of the rack of the post sliding in that guide tower, the method further comprising confirming, after releasing the release handle and before supporting the load on the equipment deck, that the locked-state flag of every locking pawl is visible through the window of the corresponding guide tower.

18. The method of claim 16, wherein the platform further comprises a crank socket coupled to the rotary input, a removable hand crank, and a mechanical interlock on the crank socket, and wherein turning the rotary input comprises inserting the hand crank into the crank socket and turning the hand crank, insertion of the hand crank being blocked by the mechanical interlock unless the release handle is held pulled, and wherein the hand crank is removed from the crank socket before the release handle is released, return of the release handle being blocked by the mechanical interlock until the hand crank has been removed.

19. The method of claim 16, further comprising placing the base frame on a support surface and mounting equipment on the equipment deck before pulling the release handle, the equipment remaining on the equipment deck while the posts move.

20. The method of claim 16, further comprising removing a removable cover of the platform and adjusting a cable adjuster of at least one of the release cables without removing the equipment deck.

