{"summary":"Scope note on what could and could not be checked. `figures/` contains only the six Markdown briefs — there is no `rendered-*.png` file for any figure, and `review/figure-audit-evidence.json` does not exist in this workspace (`review/` holds only `previous-qa.md`). No pixel-level verification of geometry, printed numerals, leaders, cutting-plane arrows or hatching was therefore possible; my figure review is brief-versus-text only. This is the root cause of both mechanical failures already reported: all 28 specification numerals are in fact allocated across the six briefs (union of the "Numerals shown on this figure" lists is exactly 10–64, matching numerals.md and the description one-for-one), and all six described figures have briefs — what is missing is the rendered sheets themselves. I have not duplicated those two checks as findings.

Source fidelity is otherwise strong. I traced every claim limitation, every numbered part and every asserted relationship to the disclosure/conversation (which are identical in substance): base frame, deck, four guide towers, corner posts, inward-facing racks, per-tower pinions, front/rear transverse shafts, two sprockets and one endless chain, worm input shaft driving a worm wheel on the front shaft, non-back-driving worm mesh that is expressly not the sole load path, pivoting locking pawls with compression-spring bias, four release cables to a base-frame-mounted release balance bar, a release handle that pivots it, the bar's slight pivot relative to the handle as the equalizer, spring return to the nearest rack tooth, locked-state flags visible through tower windows only at full engagement, the crank-socket mechanical interlock in both directions, the removable cover giving access to worm drive/sprockets/chain/pawls/cable adjusters without removing the deck, and the four variants (toothed belt, gear train, motor on the same worm input shaft, three or more than four towers). Dependent claims 2–12, 14–15 and 17–20 each map to an express disclosure sentence; "pawl pivot 42" and "balance bar pivot 50" are named articulations of relationships the disclosure states literally ("each guide tower also carries a locking pawl that pivots"; "the balance bar can pivot slightly relative to the release handle"), not new structure. No dimensions, angles, materials, percentages or experimental results were added anywhere. Terminology is consistent throughout (the brief's preserved terms are all used verbatim; claims use generic "post" while the description uses "corner post 16" for the four-tower species, with claim 3 bridging the two). No numeral labels two different parts. No [REF:] citation appears anywhere in the draft, which is correct because prior_art/INDEX.md lists no citable documents; there is consequently nothing to check under citation honesty.

I also worked the FIG. 2 / FIG. 4 section geometry through by hand and it holds: with the 4-4 arrows pointing toward the left of the plan sheet, the viewer's right-hand direction is the platform's rear, so FIG. 4's "right wall band" is the tower's rear wall — which is why the rack faces that band and the window is in it, exactly matching FIG. 2's rack "on the side of each corner post 16 that faces the interior of the base frame 10". Both endpoints carry a matching 4-4 designation with arrows in one direction and no numeral leader. Two minor items follow.","findings":[{"severity":"minor","category":"figures_and_numerals","title":"FIG. 2 brief does not separate the 4-4 cutting-plane line from the pinion at the same guide tower, while FIG. 4 shows no pinion in the sectioned space","where":"figures/FIG-2.md lines 14 and 20; figures/FIG-4.md lines 3, 8 and 12","detail":"At the lower left guide tower the FIG. 2 brief specifies two parallel front-to-rear elements without stating any lateral offset between them: the pinion, and the 4-4 cutting-plane line. FIG. 4, the view taken on that line, states that the plane passes 'through the locking pawl of that guide tower' and fills the space between the corner post and the right wall band with the locking pawl 40 alone, with no pinion 20 anywhere on the sheet (numerals listed for FIG. 4 are 14, 16, 18, 40, 42, 44, 54, 56). Because FIG. 2 leaves the line's position along the shaft unconstrained, a renderer could place the cutting plane on the pinion's mid-plane, in which case the section would have to cut the pinion in the very space FIG. 4 assigns to the pawl. This is an under-specification in the FIG. 2 brief rather than a demonstrated contradiction — with no rendered sheet available I cannot say which way it was drawn — so I mark it minor. The fix uses wording already present elsewhere in the candidate.","evidence":"FIG-2.md: "A pinion 20 is drawn at each guide tower 14 as a small wheel seen edge-on, that is, as a short bar lying across the transverse shaft to which it is fixed and running from front to rear, one end of that bar meeting the rack 18 of the corner post 16 in that guide tower 14." / "A section line 4-4 is drawn as a broken straight line lying on a vertical cutting plane perpendicular to the rack 18 of the lower left corner post 16, the broken line running from front to rear across the lower left guide tower 14 and the corner post 16 within it". FIG-4.md: "taken on line 4-4 of FIG. 2, on a vertical plane through the locking pawl of that guide tower" and "A locking pawl 40 is a solid body shown in elevation and therefore without hatching, in the space between the corner post 16 and the right wall band".","fix":"In figures/FIG-2.md line 20, constrain the line's position: after "perpendicular to the rack 18 of the lower left corner post 16", insert "and offset along the front transverse shaft 22 from the pinion 20 at that guide tower 14, the cutting plane lying on the vertical plane through the locking pawl of that guide tower,". No other file changes are needed; this matches draft/07-drawings.md line 7 and figures/FIG-4.md line 3."},{"severity":"minor","category":"disclosure_fidelity","title":"Background adds field specifics and a failure mechanism that no source states","where":"draft/05-background.md, paragraphs 1 and 5","detail":"The disclosure lists support surfaces only as "a roof, floor, vehicle bed, or other support surface" and says only that synchronization "prevents the deck from racking in the guide towers". The background supplies concrete reasons not present in the disclosure or the conversation — a roof membrane and a wash-down floor as the things a machine must stand clear of, and binding of the guided members against their guides in single-corner adjustment. prior_art/INDEX.md lists no citable documents ("| (none) | | |"), so nothing in the workspace supports these as characterisations of existing practice either. Nothing in the claims or the detailed description depends on them, so the impact is limited to unsupported assertions about the field in the background; the fix is a straight generalisation to the disclosed wording.","evidence":"draft/05-background.md: "A rooftop unit may have to stand clear of a membrane, a shop machine may have to stand clear of a wash-down floor, and equipment carried on a vehicle bed may have to stand clear of the bed itself." and "The deck twists and cocks in its guides while the adjustment is in progress, the guided members bind against their guides, and the finished height at one corner differs from the finished height at another". Disclosure: "supports a heavy machine above a roof, floor, vehicle bed, or other support surface" and "prevents the deck from racking in the guide towers".","fix":"In draft/05-background.md paragraph 1, replace the sentence with: "A machine may have to stand clear of the roof, floor, vehicle bed, or other support surface that carries it." In paragraph 5, delete "the guided members bind against their guides, and" so the sentence reads "The deck twists and cocks in its guides while the adjustment is in progress, and the finished height at one corner differs from the finished height at another, so that the machine is left out of level.""}]}
